[Music]  our basel miami is back the annual art  exhibition was canceled last year due to  the pandemic but it's in full swing this  weekend as collectors and critics look  to the next generation of emerging  artists one making a name for themselves  is kennedy yanko artist in residence at  the rubell museum and as we saw for  ourselves the work is larger than life  an artist's studio is akin to one's home  it's private  intimate and there's always house rules  sculptor kennedy yanko let our cameras  into her brooklyn studio under the  condition we didn't share any of her  secrets i'm just trying to figure out  how you connected it all is so cool good  luck  that's what i just figured out this year  you're not going to tell me no wait  her craft has evolved through her desire  to marry paint with metal  i'm really interested in challenging  perception when you look at it you don't  know if it's light or heavy or soft or  hard and as soon as you understand it  then your whole mind is flipped upside  down on its head what makes it  unique  the process i think for me you know so  much of what i'm doing is it's really  responsive a process that requires all  of her strength and patience  does it always come out the way you  expect never  i was sitting here i was expecting  something totally different for this  piece really what were you expecting  well i mean it was supposed to go in  this way and go around and work this way  born in saint louis missouri yanko grew  up in a loving home with family who  always supported her creativity her  father took her to art classes at a  young age that was how he and i  connected in a lot of ways you know he's  a painter and a colorist and that was  always just a conversation that we would  both light up about early in her process  yanko figured out how to take paint off  of canvas and turn it into a  three-dimensional material a paint skin  this piece was  after i first started working with the  skins i started using uh  this like  rubber and i would take the rubber i  would roll it out and then i would throw  the paint in the air so the paint would  atom bomb and then i would take my body  underneath the rubber and i'd kind of  expand on the way that the fractal  details interact with itself she left  home at the age of 18 first to attend  the san francisco art institute then to  participate in the living theater in new  york city they would have writers and  and actors and painters and all  different minds come together in this  space where  we could all interact and talk in  different ways that's where i started to  learn about philosophy and psychology  and what it means as an artist to  conjure emotion within and to have it  come out  and that it has to come from here first  her curiosity led her to an  apprenticeship in metal welding and the  skill sets she needed for her next  chapter people look at metal as a  man-made industrial material when i  re-contextualize it visually or in my  mind there's a sense of imbuing it with  life just just through what i'm thinking  last year during the pandemic yanko  became the first sculptor to ever earn a  residency at the rubell museum  what it was don and mara rubell own one  of the largest contemporary art  collections in the world what did you  see in her  oh my god first magic magic  we power power we literally saw one  piece and it was just so moving and you  felt the power you felt the beauty of  being able to take ordinary ugly objects  and make them beautiful and you also  felt something which you almost never  feel the ability to make a gigantic  sculpture  the opportunity gave her the tools and  space she needed to begin creating the  work she dreamed of i used over 100  gallons of paint for the skins there  because i had a room where i could do my  skins like do 40 feet of skins it was  like lay faltering for me because this  is a material that i worked with for 12  years and i'm just now beginning to  really understand it  this is the result  it's called white passing  i think making this work  was a self analysis  it is about a kind of skin what it means  to shed skin what it means to change  colors of skin  my work has is always pulled into  conversations around gender and  sexuality and identity and i haven't  addressed my racial  existence before  until she started writing the graphic  novel  indelible fluidity when i first started  writing it i had to look at my face  and i had to look at my skin and my body  in a way that i was never  forced to do before and i really had to  understand what my existence and what my  contribution to the world is as an  artist and as a person you really can't  make serious art if you're not dealing  with truth within yourself her latest  work has captured the attention of  renowned abstract artist leonardo drew  her mentor she needed to deal with these  things you know there's otherwise her  work would not have actually  transitioned to where it is right now i  don't think there is anything more  special than finding your voice is there  a destination or like your art  are you allowing life to move you in  whatever direction  you end up in i have very ambitious  projects i want to take on  um  i don't know how that's going to happen  or where or when but i trust that it  will i want to make really big  huge architectural  sculptures  like when you say big huge what do you  mean like building size  building sun yeah  building size well that blue sculpture  we saw kennedy make is for a show she's  doing next spring with cf hill in  stockholm the piece will be featured  with 19th century abstract artist hilma  off clint sound familiar when anthony  mason was doing his piece she's last  week last week right when he did that  piece he exposed us to the fact that she  may have been  the very first abstract artist on the  books but  just an incredible artist yanko is  kennedy  her first name she's incredible i loved  this was towards the end of the piece  but without having your own truth you  can't create the art that she is  creating what would it you know that's  for so many things out  there your life right and just the  notion of taking or they talked about  these pieces right taking ordinary  objects and turning them into something  sublime recycling how about that  recycling  by the way the fashion in it the two of  you phenomenal absolutely phenomenal

